**1. OVERCLAIM.** 
"NO TWO SHARE A REGISTERED PRIMARY OUTCOME FAMILY." This asserts more than the packet supports, because NCT01618305 ("Plasma HIV-1 RNA Viral Load Less Than 200 Copies/mL") and NCT01989910 ("Less Than 20 HIV RNA Copies Per ml") clearly share the virologic suppression outcome family. 

**2. UNDERCLAIM.** 
Yes. The packet shows mismatched timepoints and thresholds for the shared outcome (delivery at <200 copies vs. week 48 at <20 copies), as well as disparate patient populations (pregnant women vs. general treatment-naive patients). These are concrete, evidence-supported grounds for not pooling that the text omits.

**3. FACT vs JUDGEMENT.** 
"do not reduce to a common quantity, so there is nothing for two of them to estimate in common." This is an interpretive judgement regarding the commensurability of two different viral suppression endpoints, stated in the grammar of absolute fact.

**4. IS THE STATED REASON THE ACTUAL REASON?** 
No. The stated reason (no shared outcome family) is contradicted by the packet. The actual reason is that while two trials share the same drug contrast (Raltegravir vs Efavirenz) and the same outcome family (viral suppression), their specific measurement thresholds, timepoints, and populations differ too much to pool. The packet supports this different reason, which the text failed to give.

**5. VERDICT: REJECT.** 
Reject because the core rationale relies on a demonstrable falsehood (that no two trials share an outcome family) and fails to state the actual, evidence-backed reasons why the trials cannot be pooled.
